  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Consider two **binary** arrays AAA and BBB, each of size NNN and a permutation†^\\dagger† PPP of length NNN.\n\nYou will make two new arrays CCC and DDD, each of size NNN, such that:\n\n\nCi=APiC_i=A_{P_i}Ci​=APi​​  (1≤i≤N1 \\leq i \\leq N1≤i≤N)\nDi=BPiD_i=B_{P_i}Di​=BPi​​  (1≤i≤N1 \\leq i \\leq N1≤i≤N)\nLet us define:\n\n\nThe score of the permutation PPP as min⁡(LNDS(C),LNDS(D))\\min(\\text{LNDS}(C),\\text{LNDS}(D))min(LNDS(C),LNDS(D)), where LNDS(X)\\text{LNDS}(X)LNDS(X) gives the length of **longest non-decreasing subsequence**‡^{\\ddagger}‡ of XXX.\nF(A,B)F(A,B)F(A,B) as the **maximum** score over all possible N!N!N! permutations PPP.\nFind ∑x∈S∑y∈SF(x,y)\\sum_{x \\in S} \\sum_{y \\in S}{F(x,y)}∑x∈S​∑y∈S​F(x,y), where SSS contains all binary arrays of size N(∣S∣=2N)N(|S|=2^N)N(∣S∣=2N).\n\nSince sum might be large, please print it modulo 998244353998244353998244353.\n\n†^\\dagger† A permutation of length NNN consists of all integers from 111 to NNN exactly once. For example, [4,2,3,1][4,2,3,1][4,2,3,1] is a permutation, while [1,2,2][1,2,2][1,2,2] is not a permutation.\n\n‡^{\\ddagger}‡ A sequence AAA is a subsequence of a sequence BBB if AAA can be obtained from BBB by deletion of several (possibly, zero or all) elements. For example, [3,1][3,1][3,1] is a subsequence of [3,2,1][3,2,1][3,2,1], [4,3,1][4,3,1][4,3,1] and [3,1][3,1][3,1], but not [1,3,3,7][1,3,3,7][1,3,3,7] and [3,10,4][3,10,4][3,10,4].\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe only line of each test case contains a single integer NNN, where NNN should be the length of the binary arrays.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the sum modulo 998244353998244353998244353.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "1≤N≤20001 \\leq N \\leq 20001≤N≤2000",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1032 \\cdot 10^32⋅103."
    ],
